/-!
# Unramified algebras over a strictly henselian local ring

Let `(R, 𝔪, κ)` be a strictly henselian local ring and let `S` be a finite type `R`-algebra which
is unramified at every prime lying over `𝔪`. This file proves Lemma 04GL: `S` is `R`-isomorphic to
a product `(R ⧸ I₁) × ⋯ × (R ⧸ Iₙ) × B` with each `Iᵢ` a proper ideal of `R` and no prime of `B`
lying over `𝔪`.

The decomposition itself is Lemma 04GJ (`exists_algEquiv_prod_of_henselianLocalRing`): it produces
  `S ≃ₐ[R] (∀ i, Aᵢ) × B` with
each `Aᵢ` local and finite over `R`, and with `R → B` quasi-finite at no prime of `B` over `𝔪`.
Each factor of a product is a quotient of it, so unramifiedness along the closed fibre passes from
`S` to every factor (`isUnramifiedIn_of_surjective`), and the two halves of the conclusion are

* *the finite factors are quotients of `R`* (`surjective_algebraMap_of_isUnramifiedIn`). A local
  algebra `A` integral over `R` has `𝔪` for the contraction of its maximal ideal, so `R → A` is
  local (`isLocalHom_algebraMap`) and `maximalIdeal A` is the only prime of `A` over `𝔪`; as `A` is
  its own localization there, unramifiedness along the closed fibre *is* formal unramifiedness of
  `A` over `R` (`formallyUnramified_of_isUnramifiedIn`). Lemma 00UW then gives `𝔪A = 𝔪(A)` and
  makes `κ → κ(A)` finite separable, hence surjective as `κ` is separably closed, and Nakayama
  concludes (`surjective_algebraMap_of_map_maximalIdeal`, Lemma 00DV). The factor is then
  `R ⧸ RingHom.ker (algebraMap R A)`, a proper ideal because `A` is nonzero;
* *`B` has empty closed fibre* (`not_liesOver_of_isUnramifiedIn`). A prime of `B` over `𝔪` would be
  a prime at which the finite type algebra `B` is unramified, hence quasi-finite, contradicting the
  conclusion of Lemma 04GJ for `B`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Henselian.UnramifiedDecomposition.solution`: the statement of Lemma 04GL, as
  `Algebra.IsUnramifiedIn.exists_algEquiv_prod_quotient`.

## Implementation notes

The Nakayama step is stated for its own sake, without unramifiedness: a module-finite local
algebra `A` over a local ring `R` with `𝔪A = 𝔪(A)` and `κ(A)` generated by `κ` is a quotient of
`R`. It is the local-ring case of
`Localization.localRingHom_surjective_of_primesOver_eq_singleton`, which Mathlib states only for
the localizations at a unique prime over `p`.

Unramifiedness at a prime is `Algebra.IsUnramifiedAt`, i.e. formal unramifiedness of the
localization, so its transport along a surjection `f : S →ₐ[R] T` is formal unramifiedness passing
to the quotient `S_{f⁻¹𝔮} → T_𝔮`, which is surjective because a surjective ring map is surjective
on stalks. That is `isUnramifiedAt_of_surjective`, and it is what makes the product decomposition
usable.

For a local ring `A` the localization at its maximal ideal is `A` itself, since the complement of
the maximal ideal consists of units; `formallyUnramified_of_isUnramifiedIn` uses this to turn
`Algebra.IsUnramifiedAt R (maximalIdeal A)` into `Algebra.FormallyUnramified R A`, where the
Mathlib API of Lemma 00UW (`Algebra.FormallyUnramified.map_maximalIdeal` and the residue field
instances) applies.

## References

* [StacksProject 04GL](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04GL)
* [StacksProject 04GJ](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04GJ)
* [StacksProject 00UW](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00UW)
* [StacksProject 00DV](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00DV)
-/

open IsLocalRing



/-- Unramifiedness at a prime passes to a quotient: if `f : S →ₐ[R] T` is surjective and `S` is
unramified at `f⁻¹𝔮`, then `T` is unramified at `𝔮`.

Indeed a surjective ring map is surjective on stalks, so `S_{f⁻¹𝔮} → T_𝔮` is a surjection of
`R`-algebras, and formal unramifiedness passes along surjections. -/
private lemma isUnramifiedAt_of_surjective {R S T : Type*} [CommRing R] [CommRing S] [CommRing T]
    [Algebra R S] [Algebra R T] (f : S →ₐ[R] T) (hf : Function.Surjective f)
    (q : Ideal T) [q.IsPrime] [Algebra.IsUnramifiedAt R (q.comap (f : S →+* T))] :
    Algebra.IsUnramifiedAt R q :=
  Algebra.FormallyUnramified.of_surjective
    (Localization.localAlgHom (q.comap (f : S →+* T)) q f rfl)
    (RingHom.surjectiveOnStalks_of_surjective hf q inferInstance)

/-- Unramifiedness along the fibre over an ideal `p` of `R` passes to a quotient. -/
private lemma isUnramifiedIn_of_surjective {R S T : Type*} [CommRing R] [CommRing S] [CommRing T]
    [Algebra R S] [Algebra R T] (f : S →ₐ[R] T) (hf : Function.Surjective f) (p : Ideal R)
    (h : Algebra.IsUnramifiedIn S p) : Algebra.IsUnramifiedIn T p := by
  intro q hq hqp
  have : q.IsPrime := hq
  have : Algebra.IsUnramifiedAt R (q.comap (f : S →+* T)) :=
    h _ inferInstance ⟨by simpa [Ideal.comap_comap] using hqp.over⟩
  exact isUnramifiedAt_of_surjective f hf q

/-- The structure map of a local algebra integral over a local ring is a local homomorphism: the
contraction of the maximal ideal is maximal, hence *the* maximal ideal. -/
private lemma isLocalHom_algebraMap (R : Type*) [CommRing R] [IsLocalRing R] (A : Type*)
    [CommRing A] [IsLocalRing A] [Algebra R A] [Algebra.IsIntegral R A] :
    IsLocalHom (algebraMap R A) :=
  ((local_hom_TFAE (algebraMap R A)).out 5 1).mp
    (eq_maximalIdeal (Ideal.isMaximal_under_of_isIntegral_of_isMaximal (R := R) (maximalIdeal A)))

/-- For a *local* algebra `A` integral over `R`, being unramified along the fibre over the maximal
ideal of `R` is formal unramifiedness of `A` itself: the only prime of `A` lying over
`maximalIdeal R` is `maximalIdeal A`, and `A` is its own localization there. -/
private lemma formallyUnramified_of_isUnramifiedIn (R : Type*) [CommRing R] [IsLocalRing R]
    (A : Type*) [CommRing A] [IsLocalRing A] [Algebra R A] [Algebra.IsIntegral R A]
    (h : Algebra.IsUnramifiedIn A (maximalIdeal R)) : Algebra.FormallyUnramified R A := by
  have := isLocalHom_algebraMap R A
  have : (maximalIdeal A).LiesOver (maximalIdeal R) := ⟨(maximalIdeal_comap (algebraMap R A)).symm⟩
  have : Algebra.IsUnramifiedAt R (maximalIdeal A) := h _ inferInstance this
  have e : Localization.AtPrime (maximalIdeal A) ≃ₐ[R] A :=
    ((IsLocalization.atUnits A (maximalIdeal A).primeCompl
      fun _ hx ↦ notMem_maximalIdeal.mp hx).restrictScalars R).symm
  exact Algebra.FormallyUnramified.of_surjective e.toAlgHom e.surjective

/-- **Nakayama for a local algebra with trivial residue extension** (Lemma 00DV). Let `A` be a
local algebra, module-finite over a local ring `R`, whose maximal ideal is `𝔪A` and whose residue
field is generated by that of `R`. Then `R → A` is surjective.

Indeed `A = R·1 + 𝔪A` by surjectivity of `κ → κ(A)`, and Nakayama's lemma upgrades this to
`A = R·1` because `A` is finite over `R` and `𝔪` lies in the Jacobson radical.

This is the local-ring case of `Localization.localRingHom_surjective_of_primesOver_eq_singleton`.
-/
@[sga "3 V 4.1.3" "special case"]
private lemma surjective_algebraMap_of_map_maximalIdeal (R : Type*) [CommRing R] [IsLocalRing R]
    (A : Type*) [CommRing A] [IsLocalRing A] [Algebra R A] [IsLocalHom (algebraMap R A)]
    [Module.Finite R A] (hm : (maximalIdeal R).map (algebraMap R A) = maximalIdeal A)
    (hκ : Function.Surjective (algebraMap (ResidueField R) (ResidueField A))) :
    Function.Surjective (algebraMap R A) := by
  have hsmul : maximalIdeal R • (⊤ : Submodule R A) = (maximalIdeal A).restrictScalars R := by
    rw [Ideal.smul_top_eq_map, hm]
  refine LinearMap.surjective_of_surjective_comp_mkQ (Algebra.linearMap R A) _
    (maximalIdeal_le_jacobson _) fun y ↦ ?_
  obtain ⟨a, rfl⟩ := Submodule.mkQ_surjective _ y
  obtain ⟨x, hx⟩ := hκ (residue A a)
  obtain ⟨r, rfl⟩ := residue_surjective x
  refine ⟨r, (Submodule.Quotient.eq _).mpr ?_⟩
  rw [hsmul, Submodule.restrictScalars_mem, ← residue_eq_zero_iff, map_sub, sub_eq_zero,
    Algebra.linearMap_apply, ← ResidueField.algebraMap_residue]
  exact hx

/-- A local algebra `A`, finite over a local ring `R` with separably closed residue field and
unramified along the fibre over `maximalIdeal R`, is a quotient of `R`.

By Lemma 00UW the maximal ideal of `A` is `𝔪A` and `κ → κ(A)` is finite separable, hence surjective
as `κ` is separably closed; Nakayama then applies. -/
private theorem surjective_algebraMap_of_isUnramifiedIn (R : Type*) [CommRing R] [IsLocalRing R]
    [IsSepClosed (ResidueField R)] (A : Type*) [CommRing A] [IsLocalRing A] [Algebra R A]
    [Module.Finite R A] (h : Algebra.IsUnramifiedIn A (maximalIdeal R)) :
    Function.Surjective (algebraMap R A) :=
  have := isLocalHom_algebraMap R A
  have := formallyUnramified_of_isUnramifiedIn R A h
  surjective_algebraMap_of_map_maximalIdeal R A Algebra.FormallyUnramified.map_maximalIdeal
    (IsSepClosed.algebraMap_surjective _ _)

/-- An algebra of finite type which is unramified along the fibre over `p` and quasi-finite at no
prime over `p` has no prime over `p` at all, since an unramified prime of an algebra essentially of
finite type is quasi-finite. -/
private lemma not_liesOver_of_isUnramifiedIn (R : Type*) [CommRing R] (B : Type*) [CommRing B]
    [Algebra R B] [Algebra.FiniteType R B] (p : Ideal R)
    (hqf : ∀ (q : Ideal B) [q.IsPrime], q.LiesOver p → ¬ Algebra.QuasiFiniteAt R q)
    (h : Algebra.IsUnramifiedIn B p) (q : Ideal B) (hq : q.IsPrime) : ¬ q.LiesOver p := by
  intro hlo
  have : q.IsPrime := hq
  have : Algebra.IsUnramifiedAt R q := h q hq hlo
  exact hqf q hlo inferInstance

open IsLocalRing in
public theorem solution
    (R : Type u) [CommRing R] [HenselianLocalRing R] [IsSepClosed (ResidueField R)]
    (S : Type v) [CommRing S] [Algebra R S] [Algebra.FiniteType R S]
    (hS : Algebra.IsUnramifiedIn S (maximalIdeal R)) :
    ∃ (n : ℕ) (I : Fin n → Ideal R) (B : Type (max u v)) (_ : CommRing B) (_ : Algebra R B),
      Nonempty (S ≃ₐ[R] (∀ i, R ⧸ I i) × B) ∧ (∀ i, I i ≠ ⊤) ∧
        ∀ q : Ideal B, q.IsPrime → ¬ q.LiesOver (maximalIdeal R) := by
  obtain ⟨n, A, _, _, hAloc, hAfin, B, _, _, hBnqf, ⟨e⟩⟩ :=
    exists_algEquiv_prod_of_henselianLocalRing R S
  have _ : ∀ i, IsLocalRing (A i) := hAloc
  have _ : ∀ i, Module.Finite R (A i) := hAfin
  -- each finite local factor of the decomposition is a quotient of `R`
  have hsurj : ∀ i, Function.Surjective (Algebra.ofId R (A i)) := fun i ↦
    surjective_algebraMap_of_isUnramifiedIn R (A i) <| isUnramifiedIn_of_surjective
      (((Pi.evalAlgHom R A i).comp (AlgHom.fst R (∀ i, A i) B)).comp e.toAlgHom)
      ((Function.surjective_eval i).comp (Prod.fst_surjective.comp e.surjective)) _ hS
  -- and `B`, of finite type over `R` since it is a quotient of `S`, has empty closed fibre
  have hπB : Function.Surjective ((AlgHom.snd R (∀ i, A i) B).comp e.toAlgHom) :=
    Prod.snd_surjective.comp e.surjective
  have : Algebra.FiniteType R B := Algebra.FiniteType.of_surjective _ hπB
  exact ⟨n, fun i ↦ RingHom.ker (Algebra.ofId R (A i)), B, inferInstance, inferInstance,
    ⟨e.trans (AlgEquiv.prodCongr (AlgEquiv.piCongrRight fun i ↦
      (Ideal.quotientKerAlgEquivOfSurjective (hsurj i)).symm) AlgEquiv.refl)⟩,
    fun i ↦ RingHom.ker_ne_top _,
    not_liesOver_of_isUnramifiedIn R B _ hBnqf (isUnramifiedIn_of_surjective _ hπB _ hS)⟩

end Stacks.Henselian.UnramifiedDecomposition

/-!
# Unramified algebras over a strictly henselian local ring

Let `(R, 𝔪, κ)` be a strictly henselian local ring, that is, a henselian local ring whose residue
field is separably algebraically closed, and let `S` be an unramified `R`-algebra. Then `S`
decomposes as an `R`-algebra into a finite product of quotients of `R` and a factor whose spectrum
misses the closed fibre:
`S ≅ (R/I₁) × ⋯ × (R/Iₙ) × B` with each `Iᵢ` a proper ideal of `R` and no prime of `B` lying
over `𝔪`.

The mechanism is the decomposition of a finite type algebra over a henselian local ring into finite
local factors and a part carrying no quasi-finite point of the closed fibre (Lemma 04GJ). Each
finite local factor `Aᵢ` receives `𝔪Aᵢ` as its maximal ideal and has residue field a finite
separable extension of `κ` (Lemma 00UW); as `κ` is separably closed that extension is `κ` itself,
so `Aᵢ` is generated by `1` as an `R`-module modulo `𝔪` and Nakayama (Lemma 00DV) makes `R → Aᵢ`
surjective, i.e. `Aᵢ` a quotient of `R`. The complementary factor `B` carries no prime over `𝔪` at
all — not merely no quasi-finite one — because an unramified algebra is quasi-finite at every point
of its closed fibre.

## Main results

* `Algebra.IsUnramifiedIn.exists_algEquiv_prod_quotient`: over a strictly henselian local ring `R`,
  a finite type `R`-algebra unramified at every prime over `𝔪` is `R`-isomorphic to a finite
  product of proper quotients of `R` times an algebra with empty closed fibre.

## Implementation notes

*The factors are quotients of `R`.* The source's conclusion is a decomposition
`S = A₁ × ⋯ × Aₙ × B` in which each structure map `R → Aᵢ` is surjective; stated here is the
decomposition with `Aᵢ` replaced by `R ⧸ Iᵢ` and its canonical quotient map. The two forms are
interchangeable: a surjective `algebraMap R A` presents `A` as `R ⧸ RingHom.ker (algebraMap R A)`
by `Ideal.quotientKerAlgEquivOfSurjective`, and conversely `algebraMap R (R ⧸ I)` is surjective.
Naming the ideal is the more useful of the two, since it pins the factor rather than asserting that
some algebra with a surjective structure map exists, and it keeps the factors in `R`'s universe.
Locality of the factors, which the source inherits from Lemma 04GJ, is then free rather than an
extra conjunct: a quotient `R ⧸ I` of a local ring by a proper ideal is local
(`IsLocalRing.of_surjective'`), and `Iᵢ ≠ ⊤` is exactly the statement that the factor `Aᵢ` is
nonzero, as a local ring is.

*The hypothesis is unramifiedness along the closed fibre only.* The source assumes `R → S`
unramified, `Algebra.Unramified R S`. What its proof uses is finite type — for Lemma 04GJ — plus
unramifiedness at the primes lying over `𝔪`, and that weaker hypothesis is Mathlib's
`Algebra.IsUnramifiedIn S (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R)`. The source's version is the special case:
`Algebra.Unramified R S` gives `Algebra.FiniteType R S`, and
`fun P hP _ ↦ Algebra.unramified_iff_forall.mp ‹_› ⟨P, hP⟩` gives the hypothesis. The
generalization is genuine — a finite type algebra may be badly ramified away from the closed
fibre and still decompose, since everything outside the closed fibre is absorbed into `B` — and it
is what the two ingredients of the proof are about.

*Strictly henselian* is `HenselianLocalRing R` together with `IsSepClosed (ResidueField R)`, this
project's spelling throughout; the algebraically closed case is `IsSepClosed.of_isAlgClosed`.

*No prime of `B` over `𝔪`* is stated literally, as the negation of `Ideal.LiesOver` for the primes
of `B`. It is equivalent to `𝔪B = B` and hence to the vanishing `Subsingleton (κ ⊗[R] B)` of the
closed fibre, which is the spelling of Lemma 04GG (13); the prime form is the source's and needs no
bridge to read. It is strictly stronger than Lemma 04GJ's conclusion for the complementary factor,
which only says that `R → B` is quasi-finite at no prime of `B` over `𝔪`.

The source's `A₁ × ⋯ × Aₙ` is a dependent product over `Fin n`, with `n = 0` (so `S ≅ B`) and
`B = 0` (so `S` is a finite product of quotients of `R`) both permitted, as in the source. `B` is
quantified in `Type (max u v)`, where `S : Type v`, matching Lemma 04GJ, which is where it is
produced.

Faithfulness to [StacksProject 04GL], point by point.

1. No hypothesis is dead. `HenselianLocalRing R` fails for `R = ℂ[x]_(x)`, which is local with
   separably closed residue field `ℂ`, and there `S = R[T]/(T² - (1 + x))` is étale over `R`, is a
   domain — `1 + x` is squarefree, so not a square in `ℂ(x)` — hence indecomposable, is not a
   quotient of `R` (it is free of rank `2`), and has primes over `(x)` since
   `S/xS ≅ ℂ × ℂ`; so no decomposition exists. `IsSepClosed (ResidueField R)` fails for the field
   `R = ℝ` and `S = ℂ`, which is unramified over `ℝ` and indecomposable while `ℝ ⧸ I` is `ℝ` or
   `0`, so `S` must be `B`, which then has the prime `0` over `𝔪 = 0`. `Algebra.FiniteType R S`
   and the unramifiedness hypothesis both fail for `S = R[X]` over `R = W(𝔽̄_p)`: `R[X]` is a
   domain, hence indecomposable, is not a cyclic `R`-module, and `𝔪R[X] + (X)` is a prime over
   `𝔪`.
2. The scalars and the base are the source's. Finite type, unramifiedness and the decomposition are
   over `R` itself and never over a localisation of it: the factors are quotients of `R`, the
   equivalence is one of `R`-algebras, and the primes of `B` are contracted along
   `algebraMap R B`.
3. Where the source names a canonical map the statement pins it. The surjection `R → Aᵢ` of the
   source is not merely asserted to exist: the factor is `R ⧸ Iᵢ` and the map is the canonical
   `Ideal.Quotient.mk`. The decomposition is a single `R`-algebra equivalence, which pins the
   projections onto the factors, rather than a list of unrelated factor algebras.
4. The instantiation is not degenerate. Take `R = W(𝔽̄_p)`, the Witt vectors of an algebraic
   closure of `𝔽_p`: a complete discrete valuation ring, hence henselian (Lemma 04GM), local with
   algebraically closed residue field `𝔽̄_p`, and a domain that is not a field. Then
   `S = 𝔽̄_p × R[1/p]` is unramified over `R` — the first factor is a surjective quotient and the
   second a localisation away from an element — and the conclusion holds with `n = 1`, `I₁ = 𝔪`
   and `B = R[1/p] ≠ 0`, which has no prime over `𝔪` because every prime of `R[1/p]` avoids `p`.
   Both parts of the conclusion therefore have content here, and the instance also shows that
   `R → Aᵢ` cannot be strengthened to an isomorphism: `A₁ = 𝔽̄_p` is a proper quotient of `R`.

